/-!
# A Koszul-regular closed immersion becomes regular after a smooth cover

Let `i : Z ⟶ X` be a Koszul-regular closed immersion of schemes.  Then there is a surjective
smooth morphism `g : X' ⟶ X` whose base change `pullback.snd i g : Z ×_X X' ⟶ X'` is a *regular*
immersion.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.exists_smooth_surjective_regular_baseChange`: **Stacks tag 0692**.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.exists_smooth_surjective_regular_baseChange_of_idealSheaf`: the same
  for a closed immersion, phrased on its defining ideal sheaf.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.exists_affine_koszul_kernel`: the affine chart with a Koszul-regular
  family generating the kernel on sections, which is where the argument starts.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.isRegularImmersion_of_affine_pullback_cover`: regularity of a closed
  immersion checked on a cover of its image by affine open immersions.

## Implementation notes

The cover is built from the affine case.  Around a point of `Z` the defining ideal of `i` is, by
the affine criterion of Stacks tag 063G, generated on an affine open `U ⊆ X` by a Koszul-regular
family `f₁, …, fₙ`; since the ring of sections of `Z` over `i⁻¹U` is nonzero the ideal they span
is proper.  That criterion is available for the Koszul-regular immersions of Stacks tag 063J
part (2) through
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.isKoszulRegularIdealSheaf_iff_affine`.  Stacks tag
068Q then produces a faithfully flat smooth algebra over `Γ(X, U)` on
which the extension of that ideal is generated by a *regular* sequence — the generic
upper-triangular change of generators — and `Spec` of it is an affine smooth cover of `U` over
which the base change of `i` has regular defining ideal.  Gluing those covers to the open
complement of `Z` by a coproduct gives a surjective smooth `g : X' ⟶ X`, and regularity of
`pullback.snd i g` is checked chart by chart on the pieces.

The base-changed immersion is the canonical pullback projection `pullback.snd i g`; no
replacement up to isomorphism is needed.  Regularity of an immersion is a property of the
defining ideal of its canonical closed factor, so the last step transports it along the
isomorphism identifying two presentations of the same pullback.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Lemma 31.21.5 (Tag 0692)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0692)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 31.20.4 (Tag 063G)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063G)
* [Stacks Project, Definition 31.21.1 (Tag 063J)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063J)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 068Q](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/068Q)
-/

@[expose] public section

